<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v8:default">
<!--
    kuna: a 64-bit return whose high register the function sets to zero keeps
    all eight bytes, so the zero-extension stays in the printed C.

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 sub-variable flow, the RETURN pull
    (SubvariableFlow::try_return_pull in p3_dataflow/subflow.rs).  A strict
    fix: no option.

    Source (clang -target arm-linux-gnueabi -O2, bytes verbatim below):

        typedef unsigned long long u64;
        u64 ins16(unsigned a, unsigned short b) { return ((a + 1) << 16) | b; }
        u64 zadd(int a, int b) { return (unsigned)(a + b); }
        u64 zsh(int a) { return (unsigned)(a >> 3); }
        u64 zsel(int a, unsigned b) { return a > 0 ? b : 7u; }
        u64 zmix(unsigned a) { return a > 5 ? a : 1ULL << 40; }

    ins16 is orr r0,r1,r0,lsl #16 ; mov r1,#0 ; add r0,r0,#65536 ; bx lr.

    The bug: return recovery joins r1:r0 into one 8-byte value, and because
    r1 is the constant 0 the RETURN reads ZEXT(r0).  Dead-code analysis
    counts only the possibly-nonzero bits of a RETURN as consumed, so the
    sub-variable flow on that ZEXT trimmed the RETURN to r0 alone and the
    functions printed as `int ins16(...)`, `int zadd(...)` and `int zsh(...)`.
    A caller that widens the result sign-extends it: ins16(0x7fff, 0) is
    0x80000000 in the source and 0xffffffff80000000 through the printed
    prototype.

    The fix: a RETURN that reads a register pair no wider than eight bytes is
    not trimmed.  zmix, whose high word is not zero on every path, is the
    control and was already eight bytes wide.
-->
